---- sample-reader ----

Starting with the format_testing.zip data file:

$ unzip format_testing.zip
$ cd format_testing/ouster
$ mkdir cooked
$ cd cooked
$ cat ../lidar/20230424T060913.969605.bin | ouster-to-csv lidar --config ../../configs/config.json:ouster > 20230424T060913.969605.bin

check with:

$ cat 20230424T060913.969605.bin | csv-from-bin $( ouster-to-csv lidar --output-format ) | head

You should see reasonable timestamps as the first field.

The sample app then assumes a directory structure of:

/path/to/data
├── advanced-navigation
│   └── 20230424T060914.628500.bin
├── cameras
│   └── alvium_1800_out
│       └── 20230424T060914.204749.bin
├── configs
│   ├── config.json
│   ├── log.json
│   ├── publish.json
│   └── ros-log.json
└── ouster
    ├── cooked
    │   └── 20230424T060913.969605.bin
    └── lidar
        └── 20230424T060913.969605.bin



---- sample-reader-tcp ----

Note: this is not using comma::csv::binary_input_stream at the moment, because
it's not so easy to create a vector of them (no copy constructor) and I was
trying to make this version a bit generic. But I'll probably make another that's
specific to our situation that does use them (which then makes further
processing easier because you've already got the data structures).

$ cd format_testing

# then in three separate terminals:

$ format=$( ouster-to-csv lidar --output-format )
$ cat ouster/lidar/20230424T060913.969605.bin \
| ouster-to-csv lidar --config configs/config.json:ouster \
| csv-play --binary $format --slow 5 \
| io-publish tcp:4000 --size $( echo $format | csv-format size )

$ format=t,3ui,s[15197952]
$ cat cameras/alvium_1800_out/20230424T060914.204749.bin \
| csv-play --binary $format --slow 5 \
| io-publish tcp:4001 --size $( echo $format | csv-format size )

$ path/to/sample-reader-tcp "tcp:localhost:4000;binary=t,2uw,2ui,uw,3d,3uw,3d" "tcp:localhost:4001;binary=t,3ui,s[15197952]"
